      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, it does. The reason behind that we change a variable type from unsigned(uint32_t) to signed(atomic_t -\u003eint) for many cases. It creates differences in assembler code. I tried to analyze all files, what changes are introduced. I was focused on cortex-m.\n\nThe first type of difference is created when we compare the atomic, e.g. in mkbp_fifo.c\n```\n\tfor (i \u003d 0; i \u003c fifo_entries; i++) {\n```\nchanges from\n```\nbhi.n\t44 \u003cmkbp_fifo_clear_keyboard+0x44\u003e\n```\nto\n```\nbgt.n\t44 \u003cmkbp_fifo_clear_keyboard+0x44\u003e\n```\ncomparison for signed and unsigned.\n\nBitwise operations create another difference. I\u0027m aware that operations like ^, ~, |, and \u0026 shouldn\u0027t depend on signed/unsigned type, only \u003e\u003e and \u003c\u003c are the problem. However, e.g. I checked the task.c file for every core and it is different for each of them.\n\nThe line that makes the difference is:\n```\nchar is_ready \u003d (tasks_ready \u0026 (1\u003c\u003ci)) ? \u0027R\u0027 : \u0027 \u0027;\n```\nthe asm code is changed from:\n```\n\t\tchar is_ready \u003d (tasks_ready \u0026 (1\u003c\u003ci)) ? \u0027R\u0027 : \u0027 \u0027;\n  20:\tf8d8 2004 \tldr.w\tr2, [r8, #4]\n\t\tint stackused \u003d tasks_init[i].stack_size;\n  24:\t8938      \tldrh\tr0, [r7, #8]\n\t\t     sp \u003c (uint32_t *)tasks[i].sp \u0026\u0026 *sp \u003d\u003d STACK_UNUSED_VALUE;\n  26:\tf8d4 e000 \tldr.w\tlr, [r4]\n\t\tchar is_ready \u003d (tasks_ready \u0026 (1\u003c\u003ci)) ? \u0027R\u0027 : \u0027 \u0027;\n  2a:\t2301      \tmovs\tr3, #1\n  2c:\t40ab      \tlsls\tr3, r5\n  2e:\t4213      \ttst\tr3, r2\n  30:\t6922      \tldr\tr2, [r4, #16]\n  32:\tbf14      \tite\tne\n  34:\t2352      \tmovne\tr3, #82\t; 0x52\n  36:\t2320      \tmoveq\tr3, #32\n```\nto\n```\n\t\tchar is_ready \u003d (tasks_ready \u0026 (1\u003c\u003ci)) ? \u0027R\u0027 : \u0027 \u0027;\n  20:\tf8d8 3004 \tldr.w\tr3, [r8, #4]\n\t\tint stackused \u003d tasks_init[i].stack_size;\n  24:\t8938      \tldrh\tr0, [r7, #8]\n\t\t     sp \u003c (uint32_t *)tasks[i].sp \u0026\u0026 *sp \u003d\u003d STACK_UNUSED_VALUE;\n  26:\tf8d4 e000 \tldr.w\tlr, [r4]\n  2a:\t6922      \tldr\tr2, [r4, #16]\n\t\tchar is_ready \u003d (tasks_ready \u0026 (1\u003c\u003ci)) ? \u0027R\u0027 : \u0027 \u0027;\n  2c:\t412b      \tasrs\tr3, r5\n  2e:\tf013 0f01 \ttst.w\tr3, #1\n  32:\tbf14      \tite\tne\n  34:\t2352      \tmovne\tr3, #82\t; 0x52\n  36:\t2320      \tmoveq\tr3, #32\n```\nI still need to analyze those cases.",
